k":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.0030518162565757977,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":94.9,"_gate_insider_own":0.66982,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":100.0,"_gate_insider_buying":1.0,"_gp_insider_buying":true,"_score_insider_buying":100.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Central Bancompany, Inc. operates as the bank holding company for The Central Trust Bank that provides consumer, commercial, and wealth management products and services. It operates through three segments: Consumer Banking, Commercial Banking, and Wealth Management. The Consumer Banking segment offers consumer loans and deposit products; residential mortgage, installment lending and other consumer loan financing options; and debit and credit card loan and fee businesses. Its Commercial Banking segment provides business payment solutions including treasury management services; merchant and commercial bank card products; and banking solutions to businesses, agencies and community organizations including commercial, small business, and government. The Wealth Management segment provides wealth management solutions, including investment management, fiduciary services, financial, estate, and tax planning services to individuals, businesses, and foundations. It provides savings and checking, certificate of deposit, money market, time deposit, health savings, and interest-bearing and noninterest-bearing accounts. The company also offers commercial real estate, construction and development, commercial, financial & agricultural, multi-family and one-to-four-family residential loans. In addition, it provides wealth and cash management services; merchant services; and debit and credit cards. Further, the company offers mobile and online banking services. It operates through a network of banking offices in Missouri, Kansas, Oklahoma, and Colorado. Central Bancompany, Inc. was founded in 1902 and is headquartered in Jefferson City, Missouri.","ceo_bio":"Mr. John T. Ross | President, CEO & Director | Age 53 | Compensation: $2.5M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $350K ranks in the below the sector median among Financial Services peers. Each employee generates $79K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $1K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["Central Bancompany, Inc. is a mid-cap financial services business with a $9.0B market cap on $1.0B of annual revenue.","Returns on capital run at 10% against a 7% cost of capital, a 3-point spread that creates value, though narrow enough that competitive pressure could close it.","The business throws off $231M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 23 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders. Cash conversion is thin at 0.63\u00d7, however, which raises the oldest question in analysis: are these earnings backed by cash?.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 2.5\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Net dilution runs at 1.7% annually, transferring value from owners to stock-based comp, and insiders hold 67% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and within its sector the business captures 1.9\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 37% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.36782763810623686},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.7) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (11.9% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 20% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Large open-market insider purchases (net $1.1M) \u2014 management putting its own capital behind the business","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"CBC Pensioners Association Welcomes CBC Funding but Says Federal Budget Falls Short for Seniors","source":"thenewswire.com","link":"https://www.thenewswire.com/press-releases/1ArbFalKd-cbc-pensioners-association-welcomes-cbc-funding-but-says-federal-budget-falls-short-for-seniors.html","date":"2026-11-06","timestamp":1793987700.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.66,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Robert Ringen Hermann Jr. Donates 47,619 CBC Shares to Bob and Signa Herma